# Quality Checklist — All Three Modules Covered (Hardware, Networks, Behavioural AI)

Each checklist item is ticked, with a short, plain-language statement of what we did across all three modules.

---

☑ **The README identifies the team, module, project purpose, and locations of the materials.**
DONE. We wrote a README that lists all four members with their GitHub usernames, states that our project covers three modules — Hardware, Networks, and Behavioural AI — explains the project purpose and intended learners (beginners with basic programming literacy and no prior background in any of the three areas), and points to where each module's materials are kept.

☑ **The module outline covers the intended scope and follows a logical learning sequence.**
DONE. We produced an outline for each module, each going from simple to harder.

- Hardware: H1 from code to instructions → H2 CPU anatomy → H3 fetch–decode–execute → H4 performance basics → H5 brief intro to pipelining.
- Networks: N1 what a network does → N2 layers → N3 packets → N4 IP vs TCP → N5 DNS, routing, end-to-end trace.
- Behavioural AI: BA1 what behavioural AI is → BA2 from behaviour to data → BA3 the behavioural-AI pipeline → BA4 evaluating behaviour models → BA5 brief intro to ethics and limits.

In every module each topic builds on the previous one, and scope boundaries are written out explicitly (detailed vs. brief vs. out of scope).

☑ **Development status is clearly labelled, with gaps and uncertainties acknowledged.**
DONE. We labelled the package V1.0 and gave each section a status. For each module we stated what is complete (full explanations, worked examples, answers), what is in progress (slides and videos), and listed open uncertainties. Examples: Hardware — how deep the performance calculations should go, and whether pipelining stays in scope. Networks — the depth of TCP handshake detail, and whether congestion control stays out of scope. Behavioural AI — whether statistics go beyond confusion matrix and F1, and whether ethics is a graded goal or only awareness.

☑ **V1.0 contains substantive explanations and at least one coherent, well-developed topic—not only headings or plans.**
DONE. We wrote full prose explanations for all topics in all three modules. The most developed topic in each module is: Hardware — the fetch–decode–execute cycle (H3) with two full instruction traces. Networks — IP vs TCP (N4) with the "why both?" reasoning and a TCP/UDP contrast. Behavioural AI — the behavioural-AI pipeline (BA3) with a full engaged/disengaged worked example.

☑ **The developed material includes a worked example, an application activity or question, and an explained answer or expected outcome.**
DONE.

- Hardware — worked example: tracing `ADD R1, R2` and `LOAD R1, [0x200]` step by step with register values before and after; activity: four check-yourself questions including a trace and an execution-time calculation; explained answers: the answer key shows the expected register/PC values, the calculation steps, and why a higher clock can lose to a lower one.
- Networks — worked example: tracing a request to http://example.com from HTTP through TCP, IP, link frames, and physical bits; activity: five check-yourself questions including "compare IP and TCP"; explained answers: e.g. the Q3 answer explains that IP is best-effort routing while TCP provides reliability, ordering, and ports, and why they are kept separate.
- Behavioural AI — worked example: turning interaction logs into features, predicting "engaged/disengaged", and evaluating the result; activity: questions on extracting features, reading a confusion matrix, and spotting why accuracy misleads on imbalanced data; explained answers: the answer key walks through each step and explains the reasoning.

